"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPerformance Tuning - Spark 4.0.4 Documentation (https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.0.4/sql-performance-tuning.html)\nciteturn46view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.0.4/sql-performance-tuning.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 241\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nManage data quality with pipeline expectations | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations)\nciteturn46view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn45view4\",\"pattern\":\"fail\"}); Total lines: 295\nL158: cite13†fail | `EXPECT ... ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE`  | `dp.expect_or_fail`  | Invalid records prevent the update from succeeding. Manual intervention is required before reprocessing.\nL160: Action  | SQL syntax  | Python syntax  | Result\nL161: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL162: cite11†warn (default)  | `EXPECT`  | `dp.expect`  | Invalid records are written to the target.\nL163: cite12†drop | `EXPECT ... ON VIOLATION DROP ROW`  | `dp.expect_or_drop`  | Invalid records are dropped before data is written to the target. The count of dropped records is logged alongside other dataset metrics.\nL164: cite13†fail | `EXPECT ... ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE`  | `dp.expect_or_fail`  | Invalid records prevent the update from succeeding. Manual intervention is required before reprocessing.\nL166: ## Expectation tracking metricscite15†​ L167: \nL168: You can see tracking metrics for `warn` or `drop` actions from the pipeline UI. Because `fail` causes the update to fail when an invalid record is detected, metrics are not recorded.\nL169: \nL170: note\nL171: \nL172: For streaming tables and materialized views backed by a standalone pipeline created in Databricks SQL, the Data quality tab in the pipeline UI is not available. Query the event log to view expectation metrics. See cite16†Query data quality or expectations metrics .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nbundle command group | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands)\nciteturn46view8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn45view5\",\"pattern\":\"databricks bundle plan\"}); Total lines: 1392\nL148: For Unity Catalog resources such as schemas and volumes, pass the full name path instead of an ID. The following command binds a bundle-defined schema to an existing schema in the workspace:\nL149: \nL150: Bash\nL151: \nL152:     `databricks bundle deployment bind my_bundle_schema_ref my_existing_catalog.my_existing_schema\nL153:     `\nL154: ### databricks bundle deployment migratecite23†​ L155: \nL156: Preview\nL157: \nL158: This feature is in cite24†Public Preview .\nL159: \nL160: Migrate the bundle from using the Terraform deployment engine to using the direct deployment engine. See cite13†Migrate to the direct deployment engine . To complete the migration you must then deploy the bundle.\nL161: \nL162: You can verify a migration was successful by running `databricks bundle plan`. See cite25†databricks bundle plan .\nL163: \nL164:     `databricks bundle deployment migrate [flags]\nL165:     `\nL166: \nL167: #### Argumentscite26†​ L168: \nL169: None\nL170: #### Optionscite27†​ L171: \nL172: `--noplancheck`\nL173: \nL174:     Skip running `bundle plan` before the migration. Only applicable in Databricks CLI versions 0.280.0 through 1.4.x, which runs `bundle plan` as part of `migrate` and stops the migration if the plan reports any actions.\nL175: \nL176: cite14†Global flags L177: \nL178: #### Examplescite28†​ L179: \nL180: The following example migrates the current bundle to use the cite13†direct deployment engine :\nL181: \nL182: Bash\nL183: \nL184:     `databricks bundle deployment migrate\nL185:     `\nL186: ### databricks bundle deployment unbindcite29†​ L187: \nL188: Remove the link between the resource in a bundle and its remote counterpart in a workspace.\nL189: \nL190:     `databricks bundle deployment unbind KEY [flags]\nL191:     `\nL192: \nL193: #### Argumentscite30†​ L194: \nL195: `KEY`\nL196: \nL197:     The key of the resource to unbind\nL198: #### Optionscite31†​ L199: \nL200: `--force-lock`\nL201: \nL202:     Force acquisition of deployment lock. This option disables the mechanism that prevents concurrent deployments from interacting with each other. It should only be used if the previous deployment crashed or was interrupted and left a stale lock file.\nL203: \nL204: cite14†Global flags L205: \nL206: #### Examplescite32†​ L207: \nL208: The following example unbinds the `hello_job` resource:\nL209: \nL210: Bash\nL211: \nL212:     `databricks bundle deployment unbind hello_job\nL213:     `\nL214: ## databricks bundle-deploymentscite33†​ L215: \nL216: Preview\nL217: \nL218: This feature is in cite24†Private Preview . To try it, reach out to your Databricks contact.\nL219: \nL220: Manage the deployment metadata recorded for Declarative Automation Bundles, including deployments, their versions, the resources each deployment manages, and the operations performed on those resources. For an overview of the feature, see cite34†Bundle deployment history .\nL221: \nL222:     `databricks bundle-deployments [command]\nL223:     `\nL224: #### Available Commandscite35†​ L225: \nL226:   * `get-deployment` - Get a single deployment.\nL227:   * `get-operation` - Get a single operation.\nL228:   * `get-resource` - Get a single managed resource.\nL229:   * `get-version` - Get a single deployment version.\nL230:   * `list-deployments` - List the deployments in the workspace.\nL231:   * `list-operations` - List the operations performed in a deployment version.\nL232:   * `list-resources` - List the resources managed by a deployment.\nL233:   * `list-versions` - List the versions of a deployment.\nL235: ### databricks bundle-deployments get-deploymentcite36†​ L236: \nL237: Get a summary of a single deployment, including its latest version and Git information.\nL238: \nL239:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-deployment NAME [flags]\nL240:     `\nL241: \nL242: #### Argumentscite37†​ L243: \nL244: `NAME`\nL245: \nL246:     The resource name of the deployment, in the form `deployments/<id>`.\nL247: \nL248: #### Optionscite38†​ L249: \nL250: cite14†Global flags L251: #### Examplescite39†​ L252: \nL253: The following example gets a single deployment:\nL254: \nL255: Bash\nL256: \nL257:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-deployment deployments/abc\nL258:     `\nL259: \nL260: ### databricks bundle-deployments get-operationcite40†​ L261: \nL262: Get details for a single operation on a resource in a deployment version.\nL263: \nL264:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-operation NAME [flags]\nL265:     `\nL266: \nL267: #### Argumentscite41†​ L268: \nL269: `NAME`\nL270: \nL271:     The resource name of the operation, in the form `deployments/<id>/versions/<version>/operations/<resource-key>`.\nL272: #### Optionscite42†​ L273: \nL274: cite14†Global flags L275: \nL276: #### Examplescite43†​ L277: \nL278: The following example gets a single operation:\nL279: \nL280: Bash\nL281: \nL282:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-operation deployments/abc/versions/1/operations/my_job\nL283:     `\nL284: \nL285: ### databricks bundle-deployments get-resourcecite44†​ L286: \nL287: Get details for a single resource managed by a deployment.\nL288: \nL289:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-resource NAME [flags]\nL290:     `\nL291: #### Argumentscite45†​ L292: \nL293: `NAME`\nL294: \nL295:     The resource name of the managed resource, in the form `deployments/<id>/resources/<resource-key>`.\nL296: \nL297: #### Optionscite46†​ L298: \nL299: cite14†Global flags L300: \nL301: #### Examplescite47†​ L302: \nL303: The following example gets a single managed resource:\nL304: \nL305: Bash\nL306: \nL307:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-resource deployments/abc/resources/my_job\nL308:     `\nL309: ### databricks bundle-deployments get-versioncite48†​ L310: \nL311: Get details for a single version of a deployment.\nL312: \nL313:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-version NAME [flags]\nL314:     `\nL315: \nL316: #### Argumentscite49†​ L317: \nL318: `NAME`\nL319: \nL320:     The resource name of the version, in the form `deployments/<id>/versions/<version>`.\nL321: \nL322: #### Optionscite50†​ L323: \nL324: cite14†Global flags L325: \nL326: #### Examplescite51†​ L327: \nL328: The following example gets a single deployment version:\nL329: \nL330: Bash\nL331: \nL332:     `databricks bundle-deployments get-version deployments/abc/versions/1\nL333:     `\nL334: ### databricks bundle-deployments list-deploymentscite52†​ L335: \nL336: List the deployments in the workspace.\nL337: \nL338:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-deployments [flags]\nL339:     `\nL340: \nL341: #### Optionscite53†​ L342: \nL343: `--limit int`\nL344: \nL345:     The maximum number of results to return.\nL346: \nL347: `--page-size int`\nL348: \nL349:     The maximum number of deployments to return per page.\nL350: \nL351: cite14†Global flags L352: \nL353: #### Examplescite54†​ L354: \nL355: The following example lists the deployments in the workspace:\nL356: \nL357: Bash\nL358: \nL359:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-deployments\nL360:     `\nL361: ### databricks bundle-deployments list-operationscite55†​ L362: \nL363: List the operations performed on resources in a deployment version, such as creates, updates, and deletes.\nL364: \nL365:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-operations PARENT [flags]\nL366:     `\nL367: \nL368: #### Argumentscite56†​ L369: \nL370: `PARENT`\nL371: \nL372:     The resource name of the version, in the form `deployments/<id>/versions/<version>`.\nL373: #### Optionscite57†​ L374: \nL375: `--limit int`\nL376: \nL377:     The maximum number of results to return.\nL378: \nL379: `--page-size int`\nL380: \nL381:     The maximum number of operations to return per page.\nL382: \nL383: cite14†Global flags L384: \nL385: #### Examplescite58†​ L386: \nL387: The following example lists the operations in a deployment version:\nL388: \nL389: Bash\nL390: \nL391:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-operations deployments/abc/versions/1\nL392:     `\nL393: ### databricks bundle-deployments list-resourcescite59†​ L394: \nL395: List the resources managed by a deployment.\nL396: \nL397:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-resources PARENT [flags]\nL398:     `\nL399: \nL400: #### Argumentscite60†​ L401: \nL402: `PARENT`\nL403: \nL404:     The resource name of the deployment, in the form `deployments/<id>`.\nL405: \nL406: #### Optionscite61†​ L407: \nL408: `--limit int`\nL409: \nL410:     The maximum number of results to return.\nL411: \nL412: `--page-size int`\nL413: \nL414:     The maximum number of resources to return per page.\nL415: \nL416: cite14†Global flags L417: #### Examplescite62†​ L418: \nL419: The following example lists the resources managed by a deployment:\nL420: \nL421: Bash\nL422: \nL423:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-resources deployments/abc\nL424:     `\nL425: \nL426: ### databricks bundle-deployments list-versionscite63†​ L427: \nL428: List the versions of a deployment. Each deployment records a new version every time it is deployed, so this is the deployment's history.\nL429: \nL430:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-versions PARENT [flags]\nL431:     `\nL432: #### Argumentscite64†​ L433: \nL434: `PARENT`\nL435: \nL436:     The resource name of the deployment, in the form `deployments/<id>`.\nL437: \nL438: #### Optionscite65†​ L439: \nL440: `--limit int`\nL441: \nL442:     The maximum number of results to return.\nL443: \nL444: `--page-size int`\nL445: \nL446:     The maximum number of versions to return per page.\nL447: \nL448: cite14†Global flags L449: \nL450: #### Examplescite66†​ L451: \nL452: The following example lists the versions of a deployment:\nL453: \nL454: Bash\nL455: \nL456:     `databricks bundle-deployments list-versions deployments/abc\nL457:     `\nL458: ## databricks bundle destroycite67†​ L459: \nL460: warning\nL461: \nL462: Destroying a bundle permanently deletes a bundle's previously-deployed jobs, pipelines, and artifacts. This action cannot be undone.\nL463: \nL464: Delete jobs, pipelines, other resources, and artifacts that were previously deployed.\nL465: \nL466:     `databricks bundle destroy [flags]\nL467:     `\nL468: \nL469: note\nL485: #### Examplescite71†​ L486: \nL487: The following command deletes all previously-deployed resources and artifacts that are defined in the bundle configuration files:\nL488: \nL489: Bash\nL490: \nL491:     `databricks bundle destroy\nL492:     `\nL493: ## databricks bundle generatecite72†​ L494: \nL495: Generate bundle configuration for a resource that already exists in your Databricks workspace. The following resources are supported: cite73†app , cite74†dashboard , cite75†job , cite76†pipeline .\nL496: \nL497: By default, this command generates a `*.yml` file for the resource in the `resources` folder of the bundle project and also downloads any files, such as notebooks, referenced in the configuration.\nL498: \nL499: important\nL500: The `bundle generate` command is provided as a convenience to autogenerate resource configuration. However, if your bundle includes resource configuration and you deploy it, Databricks creates a new resource rather than updating the existing one. To update an existing resource instead, you must either use the `--bind` flag with `bundle generate` or run `bundle deployment bind` before deploying. See cite77†databricks bundle deployment bind .\nL501: \nL502:     `databricks bundle generate [command]\nL503:     `\nL519: ### databricks bundle generate appcite80†​ L520: \nL521: Generate bundle configuration for an existing Databricks app in the workspace.\nL522: \nL523:     `databricks bundle generate app [flags]\nL524:     `\nL548: #### Examplescite82†​ L549: \nL550: The following example generates configuration for an existing app named `my-app`. You can get the app name from the Compute > Apps tab of the workspace UI.\nL551: \nL552: Bash\nL553: \nL554:     `databricks bundle generate app --existing-app-name my-app\nL555:     `\nL556: The following command generates a new `hello_world.app.yml` file in the `resources` bundle project folder, and downloads the app's code files, such as the app's command configuration file `app.yaml` and main `app.py`. By default, the code files are copied to the bundle's `src` folder.\nL557: \nL558: Bash\nL559: \nL560:     `databricks bundle generate app --existing-app-name \"hello_world\"\nL561:     `\nL830: #### Examplescite100†​ L831: \nL832: The following example prompts with a list of default bundle templates from which to choose:\nL833: \nL834: Bash\nL835: \nL836:     `databricks bundle init\nL837:     `\nL838: \nL839: The following example initializes a bundle using the default Python template:\nL840: \nL841: Bash\nL842: \nL843:     `databricks bundle init default-python\nL844:     `\nL845: \nL846: To create a bundle using a custom bundle template, specify the custom template path:\nL847: Bash\nL848: \nL849:     `databricks bundle init <project-template-local-path-or-url> \\\nL850:     --output-dir=\"</local/path/to/project/template/output>\"\nL851:     `\nL852: \nL853: The following example initializes a bundle from a Git repository:\nL854: \nL855: Bash\nL856: \nL857:     `databricks bundle init https://github.com/my/repository\nL858:     `\nL859: \nL860: The following example initializes with a specific branch:\nL861: \nL862: Bash\nL863: \nL864:     `databricks bundle init --branch main\nL865:     `\nL866: ## databricks bundle opencite101†​ L867: \nL868: Navigate to a bundle resource in the workspace, specifying the resource to open. If a resource key is not specified, this command outputs a list of the bundle's resources from which to choose.\nL869: \nL870:     `databricks bundle open [flags]\nL871:     `\nL872: \nL873: #### Optionscite102†​ L874: \nL875: `--force-pull`\nL876: \nL877:     Skip local cache and load the state from the remote workspace\nL878: \nL879: cite14†Global flags L880: #### Examplescite103†​ L881: \nL882: The following example launches a browser and navigates to the `baby_gender_by_county` dashboard in the bundle in the Databricks workspace that is configured for the bundle:\nL883: \nL884: Bash\nL885: \nL886:     `databricks bundle open baby_gender_by_county\nL887:     `\nL888: ## databricks bundle plancite104†​ L889: \nL890: Show the deployment plan for the current bundle configuration.\nL891: \nL892: This command builds the bundle and displays the actions which will be performed on resources that would be deployed, without making any changes. This allows you to preview changes before running `bundle deploy`.\nL893: \nL894:     `databricks bundle plan [flags]\nL895:     `\nL896: #### Optionscite105†​ L897: \nL898: `-c, --cluster-id string`\nL899: \nL900:     Override cluster in the deployment with the given cluster ID.\nL901: \nL902: `--force`\nL903: \nL904:     Force-override Git branch validation.\nL905: \nL906: `--select strings`\nL907: \nL908:     Plan only the specified resource(s) (for example, `my_job` or `jobs.my_job`). Can be repeated or comma-separated.\nL909: \nL910: cite14†Global flags L911: #### Examplescite106†​ L912: \nL913: The following example outputs the deployment plan for a bundle that builds a Python wheel, and defines a job and a pipeline:\nL914: \nL915: Bash\nL916: \nL917:     `databricks bundle plan\nL918:     `\nL919: \nL920: Output\nL921: \nL922:     `Building python_artifact...\nL923:     create jobs.my_bundle_job\nL924:     create pipelines.my_bundle_pipeline\nL925:     `\nL926: ## databricks bundle runcite107†​ L927: \nL928: Run a job, pipeline, app, or script. If you don't specify a resource, the command prompts you to choose from the jobs, pipelines, apps, and scripts defined in the bundle. Alternatively, specify the job, pipeline, or app key or the script name declared within the bundle configuration files.\nL929: \nL930:     `databricks bundle run [flags] [KEY]\nL931:     `\nL932: ### Pass job parameterscite108†​ L933: \nL934: To pass cite109†job parameters , use the `--params` option, followed by comma-separated key-value pairs, where the key is the parameter name. For example, the following command sets the parameter with the name `message` to `HelloWorld` for the job `hello_job`:\nL935: \nL936: Bash\nL937: \nL938:     `databricks bundle run --params message=HelloWorld hello_job\nL939:     `\nL940: \nL941: note\nL942: As shown in the following examples, you can pass parameters to job tasks using the job task options, but the `--params` option is the recommended method for passing job parameters. An error occurs if job parameters are specified for a job that doesn't have job parameters defined or if task parameters are specified for a job that has job parameters defined.\nL943: You can also specify keyword or positional arguments. If the specified job uses job parameters or the job has a notebook task with parameters, flag names are mapped to the parameter names:\nL944: \nL945: Bash\nL946: \nL947:     `databricks bundle run hello_job -- --key1 value1 --key2 value2\nL948:     `\nL949: \nL950: Or if the specified job does not use job parameters and the job has a Python file task or a Python wheel task:\nL951: \nL952: Bash\nL953: \nL954:     `databricks bundle run my_job -- value1 value2 value3\nL955:     `\nL956: For an example job definition with parameters, see cite110†Job with parameters .\nL957: ### Run specific job taskscite111†​ L958: \nL959: To run only specific tasks in a job instead of the entire job, use the `--only` option with a comma-separated list of task keys. For example, the following command runs only `task_a` and `task_b` in the job `hello_job`:\nL960: \nL961: Bash\nL962: \nL963:     `databricks bundle run --only task_a,task_b hello_job\nL964:     `\nL965: \nL966: To also run a task's dependencies, add a `+` modifier to the task key:\nL967:   * Prefix a task key with `+` to also run its upstream tasks (the tasks it depends on).\nL968:   * Suffix a task key with `+` to also run its downstream tasks (the tasks that depend on it).\nL969: \nL970: For example, the following command runs `task_b` along with its upstream and downstream tasks:\nL971: \nL972: Bash\nL973: \nL974:     `databricks bundle run --only +task_b+ hello_job\nL975:     `\nL976: ### Validate a pipelinecite112†​ L977: \nL978: If you want to do a cite113†pipeline validation run, use the `--validate-only` option, as shown in the following example:\nL979: \nL980: Bash\nL981: \nL982:     `databricks bundle run --validate-only my_pipeline\nL983:     `\nL984: ### Run an appcite114†​ L985: \nL986: For cite115†Databricks Apps , `databricks bundle run` starts or restarts the app with the most recently deployed source code. Unlike a job or a pipeline, which picks up new code the next time it runs, an app is a continuously running process that keeps serving the previously deployed code until it is restarted. Always run the app after deploying so that code changes take effect:\nL987: \nL988: Bash\nL989: \nL990:     `databricks bundle deploy -t prod\nL991:     databricks bundle run my_app -t prod\nL992:     `\nL993: The `KEY` for an app is the key under `resources.apps` in your bundle configuration, not the deployed app's `name` field.\nL994: \nL995: note\nL996: \nL997: `databricks bundle run` returns as soon as the app is signaled to start. The app can still fail later during startup, for example because of a missing dependency or environment variable. To verify that a stopped app reached a running state, poll its status after the command returns. See cite116†Step 5. Wait for the app to be healthy .\nL1133: Bash\nL1134: \nL1135:     `databricks bundle run my_pipeline --full-refresh-all\nL1136:     `\nL1137: \nL1138: The following example starts or restarts an app `my_app` in the `prod` target:\nL1139: \nL1140: Bash\nL1141: \nL1161: #### Examplescite129†​ L1162: \nL1163: The following example outputs the JSON schema for the bundle configuration:\nL1164: \nL1165: Bash\nL1166: \nL1167:     `databricks bundle schema\nL1168:     `\nL1169: \nL1170: To output the bundle configuration schema as a JSON file, run the `bundle schema` command and redirect the output to a JSON file. For example, you can generate a file named `bundle_config_schema.json` within the current directory:\nL1171: \nL1172: Bash\nL1173: \nL1174:     `databricks bundle schema > bundle_config_schema.json\nL1175:     `\nL1176: ## databricks bundle summarycite130†​ L1177: \nL1178: Output a summary of a bundle's identity and resources, including deep links for resources so that you can easily navigate to the resource in the Databricks workspace.\nL1179: \nL1180:     `databricks bundle summary [flags]\nL1181:     `\nL1182: \nL1183: tip\nL1184: \nL1185: You can also use `bundle open` to navigate to a resource in the Databricks workspace. See cite131†databricks bundle open .\nL1186: \nL1187: #### Optionscite132†​ L1188: \nL1189: `--force-pull`\nL1190: \nL1191:     Skip local cache and load the state from the remote workspace\nL1192: \nL1193: cite14†Global flags L1194: #### Examplescite133†​ L1195: \nL1196: The following example outputs a summary of a bundle's deployed resources:\nL1197: \nL1198: Bash\nL1199: \nL1200:     `databricks bundle summary\nL1201:     `\nL1220: ## databricks bundle synccite134†​ L1221: \nL1222: Perform a one-way synchronization of a bundle's file changes within a local filesystem directory, to a directory within a remote Databricks workspace.\nL1223: \nL1224: note\nL1225: \nL1226: `bundle sync` commands cannot synchronize file changes from a directory within a remote Databricks workspace, back to a directory within a local filesystem.\nL1227: \nL1228:     `databricks bundle sync [flags]\nL1229:     `\nL1230: `databricks bundle sync` commands work in the same way as `databricks sync` commands and are provided as a productivity convenience. For command usage information, see cite135†`sync` command .\nL1254: #### Examplescite137†​ L1255: \nL1256: The following example performs a dry run sync:\nL1257: \nL1258: Bash\nL1259: \nL1260:     `databricks bundle sync --dry-run\nL1261:     `\nL1262: \nL1263: The following example watches for changes and syncs automatically:\nL1264: \nL1265: Bash\nL1266: \nL1267:     `databricks bundle sync --watch\nL1268:     `\nL1269: \nL1270: The following example performs a full synchronization:\nL1271: \nL1272: Bash\nL1273: \nL1274:     `databricks bundle sync --full\nL1275:     `\nL1276: ## databricks bundle validatecite138†​ L1277: \nL1278: Validate bundle configuration files are syntactically correct.\nL1279: \nL1280:     `databricks bundle validate [flags]\nL1281:     `\nL1282: \nL1283: By default this command returns a summary of the bundle identity:\nL1284: \nL1285: Output\nL1286: \nL1287:     `Name: MyBundle\nL1288:     Target: dev\nL1289:     Workspace:\nL1290:       Host: https://my-host.cloud.databricks.com\nL1291:       User: someone@example.com\nL1292:       Path: /Users/someone@example.com/.bundle/MyBundle/dev\nL1293:     Validation OK!\nL1294:     `\nL1295: \nL1296: note\nL1374:     * cite92†databricks bundle generate pipeline L1375:   * cite96†databricks bundle init L1376:   * cite101†databricks bundle open L1377:   * cite104†databricks bundle plan L1378:   * cite107†databricks bundle run L1379:     * cite108†Pass job parameters L1380:     * cite111†Run specific job tasks L1381:     * cite112†Validate a pipeline L1382:     * cite114†Run an app L1383:     * cite117†Execute scripts L1384:   * cite127†databricks bundle schema L1385:   * cite130†databricks bundle summary L1386:   * cite134†databricks bundle sync L1387:   * cite138†databricks bundle validate L1388:   * cite14†Global flags --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeclarative Automation Bundles deployment modes | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes)\nciteturn46view9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn45view3\",\"pattern\":\"production\"}); Total lines: 91\nL29: ## Production modecite7†​ L30: \nL31: To deploy your bundle in production mode, add the `mode` mapping, set to `production`, to the intended target. See cite4†bundle configuration targets mapping . For example, this target named `prod` is treated as a production target:\nL32: \nL33: YAML\nL34: \nL35:     `targets:\nL36:       prod:\nL37:         mode: production\nL38:     `\nL39: \nL40: Deploying a target in production mode by running the `databricks bundle deploy -t <target-name>` command implements the following behaviors:\n"
